% ============================================================================
% map-type-classification — the CLASSIFICATION word a source gives for a map
% type, decoded from a clause already sitting unused inside `map-type.adj`'s
% own already-quoted Geology.com source sentence (adj-facts-stdlib,
% GEOGRAPHY).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling to the already-shipped `map-type.adj` (`map_type(type,
% description)`, topographic → shows_the_shape_of_earths_surface): that
% table's own already-quoted span for the topographic row ALSO classifies
% topographic maps as a KIND of map -- a fact the type/description schema had
% no room for:
%
%     "Topographic maps are reference maps that show the shape of Earth’s
%      surface."
%
% New `map_type_classification(type, classification)` table decodes that
% span as its own row: topographic → reference_map. "Classification" (what
% KIND of map it is) is categorically distinct from the parent table's
% "description" (what the map SHOWS) -- topographic maps' description is
% shows_the_shape_of_earths_surface, but their classification as a
% reference_map is a separate fact the description column has no room for.
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? map_type_classification(topographic, $Classification)   % reference_map
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, a TWO-column table like the
% parent's own): each `row` lowers to a ground relation
% `map_type_classification(type, classification)` carrying the citation, so
% the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query -- the engine returns
% the classification WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls,
% and abstains on anything that is not the topographic type (it never
% invents a classification). The query also runs BACKWARD -- bind a
% classification and recall the map type it names:
%
%     ? map_type_classification($Type, reference_map)   % topographic
%
% A recall on `political` or `physical` -- real, already-tabled map types in
% `map-type.adj`, but whose own quoted spans ("Political Maps show
% boundaries between countries, states, counties, and other political
% units." and "Physical maps are designed to show the natural landscape
% features of Earth.") never classify the map as a KIND of map the way the
% topographic row's span does -- correctly abstains rather than inventing a
% classification for either.
%
% Provenance: the SAME sentence `map-type.adj` quotes in its own header,
% from the SAME Geology.com "Types of Maps" article that table already
% cites -- a different schema decoding a different clause of it.
%
% THIS PAIR IS NOT CURRENTLY BYTE-IDENTICAL, AND THIS NOTE USED TO CLAIM IT
% WAS. The apostrophe below was re-verified against the live page under
% #14070 and is U+2019; `map-type.adj:30` still carries the ASCII form and
% is tracked in #14111. When that lands the two agree again. The original
% claim ("byte-for-byte ... no new WebFetch") was made true by neither
% side once one side was corrected -- an identity asserted between two
% copies is only as durable as the copy nobody is editing.
%
% `trust consensus` -- the same tier the parent table already carries
% (Geology.com is a reputable secondary reference).
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table map_type_classification {
    columns type, classification

    row (topographic, reference_map)

    source "Topographic maps are reference maps that show the shape of Earth’s surface."
    locator "https://geology.com/maps/types-of-maps/"
    trust consensus
}
